#!/opt/conda/bin/python
"""rs-memory — a research agent's memory, kept as plain files the PI can read.

Everything lives under /workspace/memory/ (RS_MEMORY_DIR overrides it):

  front.md                      the front page: line of inquiry, beliefs, in
                                flight, open threads; one item per line, each
                                with an id; its number in the header; then
                                "What we know about the data", the current
                                accepted facts, written from the record at
                                every write of the page and never read back
  front/<n>.md                  every earlier front page, never deleted
  journal/<yyyy-mm>/J-<n>.md    one entry per unit of work
  staged/F-<n>.md, Q-, R-, D-   claims waiting for the PI
  record/F-<n>.md, Q-, R-, D-   accepted findings, questions, retractions,
                                facts about the data
  struck/                       staged claims the PI struck
  skills/candidates/earned-*/   proposed skills, loaded by no agent
  skills/active/earned-*/       promoted skills, copied into the agents' homes
  skills.log                    one line per skill event
  consolidated                  the last journal entry a consolidation covered
  runs/                         consolidation runs: brief, delta, log
  checks/<id>/<n>/              a finding's checks, in a researcher-subagents
                                project only: a re-run (rerun/, rerun.md) or a
                                referee run (snapshot/, verdict.md); never
                                deleted, the audit trail
  jobs/job-<n>.md               a long run the agent left going (its pid, its
                                command line, its log); marked done, never
                                deleted

That folder is the agent's own. Beside it, in the workspace, rs-memory writes
the pages the PI reads, views written from it (the store stays the single
source of truth):

  README.md                     the PI's front page: what waits for them (each
                                staged claim with its checks and the words to
                                decide it), what is running, where things
                                stand, what is known
  staging/<id>.md               one page per staged claim
  results/<id>/<id>.md          one page per accepted entry, with how it stands
                                now
  results/<id>/evidence/        a read-only copy of its evidence, made at accept
                                and never rewritten
  results/index.md              everything accepted, by topic, with its standing

Every page is rebuilt after each verb that changes what it shows, and at open
(how an existing project gets its pages). Each starts with rs-memory's marker
line; a file without it is never overwritten or removed, and is reported until
the PI moves it. A page that cannot be written never fails the verb.

Verbs:
  open [--hook]                 what to read at the start of a session
  note HEADLINE --kind K [--body TEXT]
  checkpoint ITEM...            replace the "In flight" part of the front page
  claim STATEMENT --title T --scope S --evidence FILE... [--hedge H] [relations]
        [--topic T] [--rerun CMD | --no-rerun REASON | --question | --fact]
  amend ID [--statement S] [--title T] [--scope S] [--hedge H] [--evidence FILE...]
        [relations] [--topic T] [--rerun CMD | --no-rerun REASON]
                                correct a staged claim in place
  rerun-command ID (--rerun CMD | --no-rerun REASON)
  rerun ID  /  referee ID [--agent claude|pi]
  accept ID... [--over-objection]  /  strike ID... --reason R
  view [ID | --topic T]         the state of knowledge, computed at every call
  recall WORD... [--body]
  skill propose|used|promote|retire|sync|list
  consolidate --prompt [--out FILE]  /  consolidate --apply DELTA
  close [--hook] [--agent claude|pi]
  analyst-check [--hook claude]  whether an analyst's reply names a results
                                folder holding its writeup.md (reads only)
  job add --pid N --log PATH --name TEXT [--expect-end TEXT]
  job list [--all]  /  job done ID

The checks (--rerun, rerun-command, rerun, referee, --over-objection and
accept's conditions on a finding) exist only in a project whose record,
/workspace/.orchestrator/project.json, says "subagents": true; anywhere else
every verb behaves as it always has.

A fact (D-<n>, claim --fact) is a descriptive result about the data: an
inventory line, a gap, a count. It needs a scope and evidence like a finding,
but never the checks, in any project: the PI's accept is all it takes. Its
standing is computed like a finding's.

It never deletes anything under the memory folder: a struck claim moves to
struck/, an old front page to front/, an amended claim keeps every earlier
version in its body, a job marked done keeps its file. Ids and the front page
are written under one lock file, so two sessions never hand out the same
number.

A staged claim may be amended (accepted entries are never edited: a correction
of one is a new claim that supersedes it). Its checks are bound to the hashes
of its statement, scope, hedge and evidence, so amending any of these voids
them by construction; a topic or a relation does not.

A job's state is computed at every call, never stored:
  running   its pid exists and /proc/<pid>/cmdline is byte for byte the
            command line read when it was registered (a reused pid reads as
            not running);
  ended     not running, and its log is a regular file that can be read; RS
            never saw the exit code, so this says only "the process is gone
            and its log is there", which a crash also is: the log's last line
            is shown with it, in open too, so the reader can tell;
  gone      not running, and its log is missing or not a readable file;
  done      marked with job done (whatever the process does).

Standard library only; run by RS's own interpreter.
"""
